@startuml DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM
' ==============================================================================
' HOW TO RENDER THIS FILE
' ==============================================================================
' This is PlantUML text, not an image. Options to turn it into a picture:
'   1. VS Code: install the "PlantUML" extension (by jebbs), open this file,
'      press Alt+D for a live preview.
'   2. Web: paste this whole file into https://www.plantuml.com/plantuml/uml/
'   3. CLI: `plantuml DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM.puml` (needs Java + Graphviz), or
'      `npx node-plantuml` if you prefer not to install anything locally.
' ==============================================================================
' WHAT THIS DIAGRAM SHOWS
' ==============================================================================
' The complete journey of ONE piece of data: a surveyor typing an answer into
' the electrofishing protocol form, from the keystroke in the browser all the
' way to a row updated in PostgreSQL, and the confirmation flowing back.
'
' It also shows the session/cookie check, because every single request in
' this app (including the very first page load) goes through it.
'
' Real file names are used throughout so this reads as a map of the actual
' code, not a generic textbook diagram. Where useful, the note explains WHY
' the code is shaped that way, not just what it does.
' ==============================================================================
' KEEPING THIS FILE HONEST
' ==============================================================================
' Last checked against the code: 2026-09-27, after feature 16c.
'
' Because this diagram quotes real file names, real function names and real
' constants, a rename elsewhere leaves it looking authoritative while being
' wrong. Anything that changes the automatic save, api/client.ts, the session
' dependency chain, or the order of the backend's save-time checks has to change
' this file in the same branch.
' ==============================================================================

autonumber "<b>0"
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam maxMessageSize 220
skinparam BoxPadding 10

actor "Surveyor\n(the user)" as User

box "BROWSER - React app (frontend/src)" #E8F4FD
  participant "FeldText.tsx\n(protokoll/felder/)" as Feld
  participant "React Hook Form\n(uncontrolled\nform state)" as RHF
  participant "Zod resolver\n(protokoll/regeln/schema.ts)" as Zod
  participant "useAutoSave()\n(protokoll/entwurf/useAutoSave.ts)" as AutoSave
  participant "sicherungsStore\n(entwurf/sicherung.ts\n-> localStorage)" as LocalBackup
  participant "entwurf/api.ts\n(speichereAntworten)" as EntwurfApi
  participant "apiAnfrage()\n(api/client.ts)" as Client
end box

participant "Browser\n(cookie jar + fetch)" as Browser

box "BACKEND - FastAPI (backend/app)" #FDF0E3
  participant "PUT /api/v1/protokolle/{id}/antworten\n(api/protokolle.py)" as Route
  participant "aktueller_benutzer()\n(api/abhaengigkeiten.py)" as AuthDep
  participant "lies_token()\n(security/token.py)" as JWT
  participant "AntwortenSpeichern\n(Pydantic schema)" as Pydantic
  participant "speichere_antworten()\n(protokolle/dienst.py)" as Service
end box

database "PostgreSQL\n(submissions table)" as DB

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 0. Page load - the gate every screen sits behind ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
User -> Browser: opens /protokolle/<id>/abschnitt/1
Browser -> Route: (elsewhere) GET /api/v1/ich\nCookie: befischung_sitzung=<JWT>
note right of Route
  Handled by **SitzungsWaechter.tsx** on the frontend and
  the **ich()** route on the backend. This is the exact same
  ""aktueller_benutzer"" dependency chain shown in steps 13-18
  below. If it fails, React Router renders <Navigate to="/anmeldung">
  instead of the form, and none of the steps below ever happen.
  The code is auth/useSitzung.ts and auth/SitzungsWaechter.tsx on the
  frontend, backend/app/api/abhaengigkeiten.py on the backend.
end note
Route --> Browser: 200 OK {benutzer} -> cached by useSitzung.ts\n(React Query key ['sitzung'])
Browser --> User: form renders, e.g. ProtokollFormular.tsx

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 1. Typing - purely local, no network, no re-render of the page ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
User -> Feld: types a character into an <input>
note right of Feld
  **This is "where the change is first detected".**
  ""register(name)"" in FeldText.tsx hands the <input> a ref
  straight from React Hook Form. RHF listens to the DOM's own
  native "input" event - React itself is NOT re-rendering
  anything on each keystroke. This is the "uncontrolled input"
  pattern, chosen specifically because the form has ~338 fields
  and re-rendering all of them per keystroke measured at 206ms.
end note
Feld -> RHF: native DOM "input" event updates\nRHF's internal values object
RHF -> Zod: (mode: 'onTouched') validate this field\nagainst antwortenSchema
Zod --> Feld: errors.<field> set/cleared\n-> inline red helper text
note right of Zod
  Zod is a **UX convenience only**. It never blocks a save -
  ProtokollFormular.tsx's comment says it outright: "Validity
  never reaches useAutoSave. A half-finished protocol is the
  normal state of this form and is saved exactly as typed."
  The real gate is the backend Pydantic model (step 19) and,
  for SUBMIT specifically, the domain rules in protokolle/regeln.py.
end note

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 2. The real trigger: form.watch() fires on ANY field change ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RHF -> AutoSave: form.watch(callback) fires\n(subscribed once in useAutoSave's useEffect)
activate AutoSave
AutoSave -> AutoSave: pending = true\nUI state -> {status:'saving'}\n(SpeicherAnzeige.tsx shows\n"wird gespeichert")
AutoSave -> AutoSave: (re)start an 800ms debounce timer\n(DEBOUNCE_MS constant)
note right of AutoSave
  Every further keystroke within 800ms just resets this timer
  and reuses the SAME `pending=true` flag - it does NOT queue
  extra saves. This is why typing a whole word produces one
  network call, not eight.

  Only ever one request is open at a time (the ""laeuft"" flag). A
  second save sent while the first is still open would carry the
  version the first is about to spend, so the server would refuse
  it and the tab would be told it had collided with itself.
  Anything typed meanwhile is caught by save()'s own ""finally"",
  which re-fires it.

  If the component unmounts mid-debounce (surveyor navigates away
  fast), the effect's cleanup function fires one last save. It is
  deliberately silent - reporting state into a component that is
  going away is a write to nothing - and the request may well
  outlive the page, which is exactly why the local copy in step 3
  is written BEFORE the request rather than after it fails.

  ""SPEICHERT"" and its siblings are module-level constants, not
  fresh object literals. setState re-renders whenever the new value
  is not Object.is-equal to the old one, so a literal here
  re-rendered ProtokollFormular and the whole open section on every
  keystroke: measured at 206ms per keystroke on 2026-09-04 with all
  26 catch rows filled. Reusing one object lets React bail out.
end note

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 3. Debounce elapses -> the actual save runs ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AutoSave -> RHF: form.getValues()\n(reads the WHOLE Antworten document,\nnot just the changed field)
RHF --> AutoSave: the full answers object
AutoSave -> LocalBackup: sicherungsStore.schreib({id, version, antworten})
note right of LocalBackup
  Written to **localStorage BEFORE the network call**, every
  single time, on purpose. If the request never arrives (closed
  laptop, dead wifi, killed tab) this copy is the only place the
  typing still exists. It is only deleted once the server has
  confirmed the save (step 32) - see SicherungAngebot.tsx, which
  offers to restore this copy if the page is reopened and it is
  still there.
end note

alt protocol has no server record yet (opened via "Neues Protokoll")
  AutoSave -> EntwurfApi: legeEntwurfAn()
  EntwurfApi -> Client: apiAnfrage('/protokolle', {methode:'POST'})
  Client -> Browser: fetch('/api/v1/protokolle', {credentials:'same-origin'})
  Browser -> Route: POST /api/v1/protokolle\nCookie: befischung_sitzung=<JWT>
  Route --> Browser: 201 {id, version} (a real database row now exists)
  Browser --> Client: parsed JSON
  Client --> EntwurfApi: Entwurf{id, version}
  EntwurfApi --> AutoSave: real id + version\n(replaces the placeholder "neu")
end

AutoSave -> EntwurfApi: speichereAntworten({id, version, antworten})
EntwurfApi -> Client: apiAnfrage(`/protokolle/${id}/antworten`,\n{methode:'PUT', koerper:{version, antworten}})

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 4. Leaving the browser: the ONE place a fetch() call is allowed ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
note over Client
  **api/client.ts is the only file that calls fetch().**
  Every other file (entwurf/api.ts, useSitzung.ts,
  etc.) goes through apiAnfrage(). That is what keeps 4 concerns
  centralised instead of repeated at every call site:
  (a) the cookie is sent, (b) a non-2xx response becomes a typed
  ApiFehler instead of a silently-resolved promise, (c) the JSON
  body is stringified, (d) an unreadable response is told apart
  from a working one.
end note
Client -> Browser: fetch('/api/v1/protokolle/{id}/antworten', {\n  method: 'PUT',\n  credentials: 'same-origin',   <-- attaches the cookie\n  headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},\n  body: JSON.stringify({version, antworten})\n})
Browser -> Browser: browser automatically reads\nthe httpOnly "befischung_sitzung" cookie\nfrom its cookie jar and attaches it
note right of Browser
  **JavaScript never touches this cookie.** httpOnly means
  document.cookie cannot see it, so nothing in the code above
  this line (not even api/client.ts) ever handles the JWT
  directly - the browser does it invisibly because of
  credentials:'same-origin'. This is the whole point of the
  httpOnly design: a compromised npm package could still make
  requests, but could never read and exfiltrate the token.
end note
Browser -> Route: HTTP PUT /api/v1/protokolle/{id}/antworten\nCookie: befischung_sitzung=<JWT>\nContent-Type: application/json\nBody: {"version": 7, "antworten": {...338 fields...}}

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 5. Backend: authenticate FIRST, before any route code runs ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Route -> AuthDep: FastAPI runs this Depends()\nBEFORE the route body
activate AuthDep
AuthDep -> AuthDep: read the "befischung_sitzung"\ncookie value
alt no cookie present
  AuthDep --> Route: raise NichtAngemeldet -> 401\n(route body NEVER executes)
end
AuthDep -> JWT: lies_token(cookie_value)
JWT -> JWT: verify HMAC-SHA256 signature\nwith JWT_SECRET, check exp not passed
alt signature invalid / forged / expired / malformed
  JWT --> AuthDep: raises TokenFehler subtype
  AuthDep --> Route: re-raised as NichtAngemeldet -> 401
end
JWT --> AuthDep: uuid of the account ("sub" claim)
AuthDep -> DB: session.get(User, benutzer_id)\n<b>fresh read, every single request</b>
DB --> AuthDep: the User row (email, rollen, ist_aktiv, ...)
alt account deleted or ist_aktiv == False
  AuthDep --> Route: raise NichtAngemeldet -> 401
end
AuthDep --> Route: authenticated `benutzer: User`\ninjected as a normal function parameter
deactivate AuthDep
note right of AuthDep
  Roles are **not** read from the JWT - only the account id is.
  Everything else (roles, active flag) is re-read from Postgres
  on every request, which is why deactivating someone or removing
  a role takes effect on their very next click, not after their
  token eventually expires (up to 8 hours later).
end note

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 6. Structural validation, then business rules, then the write ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Route -> Pydantic: FastAPI parses the JSON body\ninto AntwortenSpeichern(version:int, antworten:dict)
alt body does not match the schema (wrong types, missing fields)
  Pydantic --> Route: 422 ANFRAGE_UNGUELTIG\n(handled centrally by api/fehler_http.py)
end
Pydantic --> Route: a typed, structurally-valid request object

Route -> Service: speichere_antworten(session,\n  protokoll_id, besitzer=benutzer,\n  antworten, version)
activate Service
Service -> DB: hole_protokoll():\nSELECT * FROM submissions\nWHERE id = :protokoll_id\n  AND owner_user_id = :besitzer.id
note right of Service
  **Ownership is a WHERE clause, not an "if" after the fetch.**
  If the id belongs to someone else, this query simply returns
  no row - the API answers 404 PROTOKOLL_NICHT_GEFUNDEN, the exact
  same answer as if the id never existed at all, so a stranger
  can never learn "this id is real, I just can't see it."

  A **save** looks the protocol up by owner, which is what this
  query does. **Reading** one goes through a wider clause,
  ""_sichtbar()"" in the same file, so a Reviewer, Data Steward or
  Super Admin can open a protocol that has been handed in. A draft
  stays private to its owner either way.
end note
DB --> Service: the Submission row, or none
alt no matching row
  Service --> Route: raise ProtokollNichtGefunden -> 404
end
note over Service
  From here the three checks and the two writes are
  ""uebernimm_antworten()"", split out in feature 23b so the PDF
  import stores its answers down this same path rather than
  beside it. The order below is deliberate and is documented in
  the function's own docstring.
end note
Service -> Service: pruefe_aenderbar(protokoll.status)\n(only DRAFT and NEEDS_CHANGES may be written to)
alt already submitted, accepted or locked
  Service --> Route: raise ProtokollNichtMehrEntwurf -> 409
end
Service -> Service: pruefe_version(): compare given `version` (7)\nto row.version (must match)
alt version mismatch (somebody else saved since this tab loaded)
  Service --> Route: raise ProtokollVeraendert -> 409\n<b>nothing is written</b>
  note left of Service
    Optimistic concurrency control. The frontend's SaveState
    gets a dedicated 'conflict' status (not just 'failed') because
    retrying can never succeed - see fehlerZustand() in
    useAutoSave.ts - and the surveyor is told to reload the page.

    On a conflict useAutoSave sets its ""aufgegeben"" flag and stops
    sending requests entirely: every later save would carry the same
    stale version and be refused the same way, which would only
    overwrite the one message worth reading. It keeps writing the
    localStorage copy on every keystroke, though, because
    SpeicherProblem.tsx promises the surveyor their entries are safe
    in this browser, and that promise has to stay true.
  end note
end
Service -> Service: pruefe_antworten(): check the document itself\n(protokolle/regeln.py)
note right of Service
  Three things, in this order (the order is in the docstring):
  (a) it is a JSON object at all, else ANTWORTEN_NICHT_LESBAR 422;
  (b) it is under MAX_ZEICHEN_GESAMT = 200_000 characters, else
      ANTWORTEN_ZU_GROSS 413 - checked before the field rules so a
      runaway document does not produce a violation list as long as
      itself;
  (c) ""finde_verstoesse()"" walks every key against the paths the
      form version declares (""formular().pfade"", read from
      database/seed/ - see ADR 0003/0004), rejecting unknown paths,
      dotted key names and over-deep nesting, else
      ANTWORTEN_UNGUELTIG 422 carrying a ""verstoesse"" list the
      screen draws field by field.
  Walked with an explicit stack, not recursion: the document is
  untrusted input and a recursive walk over untrusted nesting is a
  stack overflow waiting to be found.
end note
Service -> DB: UPDATE submissions\nSET antworten = :neu, version = version + 1,\n    updated_at = now()\nWHERE id = :protokoll_id
note right of Service
  ""antworten"" is **assigned**, never edited in place. SQLAlchemy
  notices an attribute being set; it does not watch the inside of a
  plain dict, so mutating the stored document would leave a save
  that quietly wrote nothing.

  ""version"" is raised on every save, even one whose answers are
  identical, or the client's next save would arrive claiming a
  version already behind. ""updated_at"" comes from the model's
  ""onupdate=func.now()"", so the database's clock stamps it rather
  than this process's.
end note
DB --> Service: updated row (new version, new updated_at)
Service --> Route: the updated Submission
deactivate Service

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 7. Response flows back up, UI reflects the confirmed save ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Route --> Browser: 200 OK\n{"version": 8, "updated_at": "2026-09-21T..."}
Browser --> Client: Response object
Client --> EntwurfApi: parsed SpeicherAntwort{version, updated_at}
EntwurfApi --> AutoSave: resolved promise
AutoSave -> LocalBackup: sicherungsStore.loesche(id)\n<b>only now</b> is the safety copy removed
AutoSave -> AutoSave: version.current = 8\nsetState({status:'saved', zeitpunkt: updated_at})
AutoSave --> User: SpeicherAnzeige.tsx / ProtokollKopf.tsx\nre-render: "Gespeichert um 14:32"
note right of AutoSave
  Two components read this one SaveState, and they say different
  things. **SpeicherAnzeige.tsx** is the short indicator, drawn
  twice on the page (head and foot of the card, only one of them an
  ARIA live region, or every save would be announced twice).
  **SpeicherProblem.tsx** appears only on 'failed' or 'conflict' and
  has room to say what happened and what to do about it.
end note
deactivate AutoSave

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== 8. The two ways out of the debounce: the banner, and submitting ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
note across
  The hook exposes two escape hatches from the 800ms wait, and they exist for
  different reasons.

  **""jetztSpeichern()""** is the restore banner's (SicherungAngebot.tsx). When a
  surveyor accepts the localStorage copy from a previous sitting, the answers are
  put into the form and saved immediately rather than after a debounce nobody is
  typing into.

  **""bereitZumAbsenden()""** is the submit button's (absenden/useAbsenden.ts). A
  submit sends **no answers of its own**: whatever is stored is what gets
  submitted. So anything still sitting in the debounce would simply be left
  behind, and somebody who types into the last field and presses the button
  straight away would submit the protocol as it was one keystroke ago. It waits
  for the saving to come to rest, in a LOOP rather than awaiting one save, because
  save() re-fires itself when something was typed while a request was open and the
  submit has to follow the last of those rather than the first. It answers with
  the version the protocol is now at, or **null** when the last save failed, so
  the caller refuses to submit rather than sending a version it knows is wrong.
end note

' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
== Error paths, shown separately for clarity ==
' ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
note across
  **Network failure** (fetch itself rejects - no response at all, e.g. wifi
  dropped): api/client.ts throws ApiFehler(NETZWERK_FEHLER). useAutoSave's
  catch block calls fehlerZustand(), which maps this to {status:'failed'}.
  The local backup from step 3 is deliberately **kept**, and a future
  keystroke or the next debounce cycle will simply try saving again.

  **502/503/504 from a reverse proxy** (backend container restarting):
  api/client.ts recognises these specific statuses and reports the same
  NETZWERK_FEHLER, so it is never confused with "your password is wrong"
  or any other application-level refusal.

  **401 from ANY call, not just /ich**: useSitzung's cache flips to
  {zustand:'abgemeldet', grund:'antwort'}, and the NEXT time
  SitzungsWaechter renders (e.g. user clicks any nav link) they are
  redirected to /anmeldung?weiter=<where they were>&grund=abgelaufen.
  The in-flight autosave itself just reports {status:'failed'} - it does
  not redirect anything by itself, since a background save failing is not
  the moment to yank a half-typed answer out from under someone.
end note

@enduml
